"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[39256],{939256:e=>{e.exports="# Objects & DDL\n\nSnowflake → DBSQL equivalents for schema objects, table definitions (including `TEMPORARY` and `TRANSIENT`), constraints, clustering, sequences, identity, materialized views, secure views, and row-access / masking policies.\n\n> Time Travel, retention tuning, and zero-copy `CLONE` live in `references/snowflake/06-time-travel-and-clones.md`.\n\n> **Databricks defaults**: All managed tables are **Delta format** by default — no `USING DELTA` clause is required unless overriding. Reference permanent objects using **Unity Catalog three-part names**: `catalog.schema.table`.\n\n## SNOWFLAKE\n\n### IDENTITY / AUTOINCREMENT columns\n\n> DBSQL identity column DDL is shown inline below. Load `skills/writing-sql/table-optimization.md` if you need clustering or partitioning context alongside DDL changes.\n\nSnowflake supports both `IDENTITY(start, increment)` and `AUTOINCREMENT START n INCREMENT m` — they are synonyms.\n\n**WARNING: Identity columns have significant implications in Databricks.**\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nCREATE TABLE t (\n    id NUMBER AUTOINCREMENT START 1 INCREMENT 1,\n    name VARCHAR(100)\n);\n-- Or equivalent:\nCREATE TABLE t (\n    id NUMBER IDENTITY(1,1),\n    name VARCHAR(100)\n);\n\n-- DBSQL (Delta, Unity Catalog)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.t (\n    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY,\n    name STRING\n);\n```\n\n**Critical limitations (same as all Delta identity columns):**\n\n1. **Disables parallel writes** — Tables with identity columns cannot be written concurrently\n2. **Values not guaranteed sequential** — May have gaps; not in insertion order\n3. **Cannot be added later** — Cannot add identity columns via `ALTER TABLE`\n4. **Type**: Snowflake `NUMBER AUTOINCREMENT` permits up to 38 digits; DBSQL `GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY` requires `BIGINT` (64-bit signed). If the source used more than 19 digits, use `UUID()` or a custom scheme instead.\n\n**Preferred alternative — UUID (allows parallel writes):**\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.t (id STRING DEFAULT UUID(), name STRING);\n```\n\n**Snowflake `ORDER` / `NOORDER` clause**: Snowflake's `AUTOINCREMENT ... ORDER` forces strictly monotonic values (with performance cost); `NOORDER` (default) allows gaps for concurrency. DBSQL identity columns behave like Snowflake `NOORDER` — values are generated in parallel, ordering is not guaranteed. Drop the `ORDER`/`NOORDER` clause during migration.\n\n### Indexes and Constraints\n\nSnowflake standard tables: `PRIMARY KEY`, `UNIQUE`, `FOREIGN KEY` — **informational only** (not enforced). `NOT NULL` and `CHECK` constraints — **enforced**. Snowflake does not support classical secondary indexes.\n\nSnowflake hybrid tables (different table kind): `PRIMARY KEY`, `UNIQUE`, `FOREIGN KEY`, `NOT NULL` — **all enforced**. `CHECK` — not supported. If the source uses hybrid tables, flag: the enforced PK/UK/FK behavior does not carry over to DBSQL.\n\nDBSQL: Primary keys, foreign keys, and unique constraints exist as **informational only** (not enforced) — matches Snowflake standard tables; requires explicit `NOT ENFORCED` keyword. `NOT NULL` and `CHECK` constraints **are enforced on write** — matches Snowflake standard tables.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake (standard table): informational FK, enforced CHECK\nALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT fk_customer FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES customers(id);\nALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT chk_amount CHECK (amount > 0);\n\n-- DBSQL: informational FK (explicit NOT ENFORCED required), enforced CHECK\nALTER TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.orders ADD CONSTRAINT fk_customer\n  FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES my_catalog.my_schema.customers(id) NOT ENFORCED;\nALTER TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.orders ADD CONSTRAINT chk_amount CHECK (amount > 0);\n```\n\n**Migration implications:**\n\n- Standard-table CHECK constraints: already enforced on both sides — behavior preserved.\n- Hybrid-table PK/UK/FK: enforced on Snowflake, informational on DBSQL. If the source application relied on hybrid-table enforcement, application-level deduplication / integrity checks are needed after migration.\n\n### ALTER TABLE — column changes\n\n| Snowflake                                                   | DBSQL                                                           | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `ALTER TABLE t ADD COLUMN col NUMBER`                       | `ALTER TABLE t ADD COLUMN col DECIMAL(38,0)`                    | Snowflake NUMBER default precision is (38,0)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| `ALTER TABLE t ADD COLUMN col NUMBER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0`    | `ALTER TABLE t ADD COLUMN col DECIMAL(38,0) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0` | Delta supports NOT NULL + DEFAULT on new columns                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN col SET DATA TYPE NUMBER(38,5)` | **Partial support**                                             | Arbitrary type changes not supported on Delta. **Widening** changes (e.g. `INT` → `BIGINT`, `DECIMAL` precision increase, `DATE` → `TIMESTAMP_NTZ`) work via `ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE` when the `typeWidening` table feature is enabled. For non-widening changes: cast at query time, or recreate the table. |\n| `ALTER TABLE t DROP COLUMN col`                             | `ALTER TABLE t DROP COLUMN col`                                 | Supported in Delta; data retained until `REORG TABLE ... APPLY (PURGE)`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| `ALTER TABLE t RENAME COLUMN old TO new`                    | `ALTER TABLE t RENAME COLUMN old TO new`                        | Supported                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| `ALTER TABLE t SWAP WITH other_table`                       | **No direct equivalent**                                        | Use `CREATE OR REPLACE` with a select from the source, or rename-pair pattern (`ALTER TABLE t RENAME TO t_old; ALTER TABLE staging RENAME TO t;`)                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY (col)`                            | `ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY (col)`                                | Both dialects — Liquid Clustering in DBSQL                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| `ALTER TABLE t DROP CLUSTERING KEY`                         | `ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY NONE`                                 |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `ALTER TABLE t SUSPEND RECLUSTER` / `RESUME RECLUSTER`      | **No equivalent**                                               | DBSQL Liquid Clustering has no pause/resume; background optimization is automatic                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake: change column type (widening)\nALTER TABLE orders ALTER COLUMN notes SET DATA TYPE VARCHAR(4000);\n\n-- DBSQL: no ALTER COLUMN SET DATA TYPE on Delta. Alternatives:\n-- Option 1: cast at query time (non-destructive)\nSELECT CAST(notes AS STRING) AS notes FROM my_catalog.my_schema.orders;\n-- Option 2: recreate with new type (destructive)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.orders_new AS\nSELECT *, CAST(notes AS STRING) AS notes_v2 FROM my_catalog.my_schema.orders;\n```\n\n### DEFAULT constraints\n\n| Snowflake DEFAULT                                | DBSQL equivalent                                          |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()`                    | `DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP`                               |\n| `DEFAULT CURRENT_DATE()`                         | `DEFAULT CURRENT_DATE`                                    |\n| `DEFAULT UUID_STRING()`                          | `DEFAULT UUID()`                                          |\n| `DEFAULT my_seq.NEXTVAL`                         | `DEFAULT (NEXT VALUE FOR my_catalog.my_schema.my_seq)`    |\n| `DEFAULT 0` / `DEFAULT ''` / `DEFAULT 'pending'` | Same — literals are identical                             |\n| `DEFAULT SYSDATE()`                              | `DEFAULT UTC_TIMESTAMP()` (Snowflake SYSDATE returns UTC) |\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nCREATE TABLE orders (\n    order_id   NUMBER     AUTOINCREMENT,\n    created_at TIMESTAMP  DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP(),\n    status     VARCHAR    DEFAULT 'pending',\n    is_active  BOOLEAN    DEFAULT TRUE\n);\n\n-- DBSQL\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.orders (\n    order_id   BIGINT     GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY,\n    created_at TIMESTAMP  DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n    status     STRING     DEFAULT 'pending',\n    is_active  BOOLEAN    DEFAULT true\n);\n```\n\n### Clustering keys\n\nSnowflake clustering keys and DBSQL Liquid Clustering use the **same `CLUSTER BY` keyword** but have different implementations:\n\n- Snowflake auto-reclusters in the background; cost is charged as a separate service\n- DBSQL Liquid Clustering (GA on DBR 15.2+; write-only preview on DBR 13.3+) optimizes on write and via explicit `OPTIMIZE` commands\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nCREATE TABLE fact_sales (region VARCHAR, order_date DATE, amount NUMBER)\nCLUSTER BY (region, order_date);\n\n-- DBSQL (Liquid Clustering — recommended)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.fact_sales (region STRING, order_date DATE, amount DECIMAL(18,2))\nCLUSTER BY (region, order_date);\n\n-- DBSQL alternative for legacy / Hive-partitioned tables: partition + Z-ORDER\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.fact_sales (region STRING, order_date DATE, amount DECIMAL(18,2))\nPARTITIONED BY (order_date);\nOPTIMIZE my_catalog.my_schema.fact_sales ZORDER BY (region);\n```\n\nSee `references/snowflake/08-performance.md` for when to choose Liquid Clustering vs partition + Z-ORDER vs plain Delta.\n\n### SEQUENCE objects\n\nBoth Snowflake and DBSQL support sequences. Reference syntax differs.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nCREATE SEQUENCE my_seq START = 1000 INCREMENT = 1;\nSELECT my_seq.NEXTVAL;\nSELECT my_seq.NEXTVAL, my_seq.NEXTVAL FROM (SELECT 1) t;\n\n-- DBSQL (Unity Catalog three-part name)\nCREATE SEQUENCE my_catalog.my_schema.my_seq START WITH 1000 INCREMENT BY 1;\nSELECT NEXT VALUE FOR my_catalog.my_schema.my_seq;\n```\n\n**Differences:**\n\n- Snowflake uses `seq.NEXTVAL` (dot notation, pseudo-column). DBSQL uses `NEXT VALUE FOR catalog.schema.seq`.\n- Snowflake `ORDER` / `NOORDER` — DBSQL does not guarantee ordering; drop the clause.\n- Snowflake accepts `START N`, `START WITH N`, or `START = N` — all equivalent. DBSQL requires `START WITH N` (no bare or `=` form).\n- Snowflake `INCREMENT N` / `INCREMENT = N` / `INCREMENT BY N` → DBSQL requires `INCREMENT BY N`.\n- Snowflake default `NOORDER` maps naturally; just use DBSQL defaults.\n\n### Table persistence variants — PERMANENT / TRANSIENT / TEMPORARY\n\nSnowflake defines three persistence variants that DBSQL collapses into a smaller set:\n\n| Snowflake                                                                 | Persistence                                   | DBSQL equivalent                                                                                                                            |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `CREATE TABLE t (...)` (default: PERMANENT)                               | Persistent; full Time Travel + Fail-safe      | `CREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.t (...)` — Delta, configurable retention                                                                 |\n| `CREATE TRANSIENT TABLE t (...)`                                          | Persistent; no Fail-safe, 0–1 day Time Travel | `CREATE TABLE ...` with reduced `delta.*RetentionDuration` (see below)                                                                      |\n| `CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE t (...)` / `CREATE LOCAL TEMPORARY TABLE t (...)` | Session-scoped; dropped when session ends     | `CREATE TEMP TABLE` (SQL warehouse only) **or** `CREATE OR REPLACE TEMPORARY VIEW` (all compute) **or** UC staging table with run-id suffix |\n\n**TRANSIENT — reduced-retention Delta:**\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nCREATE TRANSIENT TABLE my_db.my_schema.t (...);\n\n-- DBSQL: regular Delta, reduced retention if needed\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.t (...);\nALTER TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.t SET TBLPROPERTIES (\n  'delta.logRetentionDuration' = 'interval 1 days',\n  'delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration' = 'interval 1 days'\n);\n```\n\nDBSQL has no \"Fail-safe\" concept (the additional Snowflake recovery period after Time Travel ends has no analog).\n\n**TEMPORARY — session-scoped tables:**\n\nDBSQL `CREATE TEMP TABLE` is the closest direct mapping but has critical limitations:\n\n1. **SQL warehouse sessions only** — not supported in notebook or jobs contexts running on classic or serverless compute clusters.\n2. **No `ALTER TABLE`** — cannot add/drop columns after creation.\n3. **Session-scoped** — dropped automatically when the session ends.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nCREATE TEMPORARY TABLE staging AS\nSELECT * FROM source WHERE active = TRUE;\n\n-- DBSQL (SQL warehouse only)\nCREATE TEMP TABLE staging AS\nSELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.source WHERE active = TRUE;\n```\n\n**Preferred DBSQL alternatives** (in order of flexibility):\n\n```sql\n-- CTE (best for single-query scope)\nWITH staging AS (\n  SELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.source WHERE active = TRUE\n)\nSELECT ... FROM staging;\n\n-- Temporary view (session-scoped; works on ALL compute types)\nCREATE OR REPLACE TEMPORARY VIEW staging AS\nSELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.source WHERE active = TRUE;\n\n-- Permanent staging table in Unity Catalog (most flexible for multi-step ETL)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.staging.orders_staging AS\nSELECT ... FROM my_catalog.my_schema.source;\n-- ... use across multiple statements / sessions ...\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS my_catalog.staging.orders_staging;\n```\n\n**Cross-session staging** — Snowflake does not have cross-session shared temp tables. Pattern in DBSQL is a permanent staging table with a unique name per pipeline run (e.g. using `{{job.run_id}}` as a suffix), then `DROP TABLE` at the end:\n\n```sql\n-- Databricks Workflow\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.staging.orders_run_12345 AS\nSELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.source WHERE condition;\n-- ... shared across downstream tasks in the workflow ...\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS my_catalog.staging.orders_run_12345;\n```\n\nIf a run id is not available, generate a UUID before the pipeline starts and pass it as a parameter to all tasks that share the staging table.\n\n### CTAS / CREATE TABLE LIKE\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake: CTAS with clustering key\nCREATE TABLE summary CLUSTER BY (region) AS\nSELECT region, SUM(amt) FROM my_catalog.my_schema.orders GROUP BY region;\n\n-- DBSQL (Liquid Clustering)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.summary CLUSTER BY (region) AS\nSELECT region, SUM(amt) FROM my_catalog.my_schema.orders GROUP BY region;\n\n-- Snowflake: CREATE TABLE LIKE (copy schema, no data)\nCREATE TABLE staging LIKE source;\n\n-- DBSQL (DBR 13.3 LTS+): same LIKE syntax\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.staging LIKE my_catalog.my_schema.source;\n\n-- DBSQL (older runtimes): zero-row CTAS\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.staging AS\nSELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.source WHERE 1 = 0;\n```\n\n### External tables and stages\n\nSnowflake `CREATE STAGE` + `CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE` → DBSQL UC External Location + Volume + either `COPY INTO` managed table, or external Delta table.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nCREATE STAGE my_stage URL = 's3://mybucket/data/' STORAGE_INTEGRATION = my_int;\nCREATE FILE FORMAT my_parquet TYPE = PARQUET;\nCREATE EXTERNAL TABLE ext_sales (...)\nWITH LOCATION = @my_stage FILE_FORMAT = my_parquet;\n\n-- DBSQL option 1: managed table + COPY INTO (preferred for recurring loads)\n-- (UC external location and credential set up once, outside this SQL.)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.ext_sales (...);\nCOPY INTO my_catalog.my_schema.ext_sales\nFROM 's3://mybucket/data/'\nFILEFORMAT = PARQUET\nPATTERN = '*.parquet';\n\n-- DBSQL option 2: external Delta table (file-level sharing, read-only proxy)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.ext_sales\nUSING PARQUET LOCATION 's3://mybucket/data/';\n```\n\n> For full `COPY INTO`, external location, and volume syntax, load `skills/writing-sql/foreign-tables.md`.\n\nDeeper pipeline patterns (Snowpipe, streams, tasks) are in `references/snowflake/07-pipelines.md`.\n\n### Views — column-list / SELECT must stay in sync (CRITICAL)\n\n`CREATE VIEW (col_a, col_b, col_c) AS SELECT ...` is valid in both Snowflake and DBSQL. DBSQL's documented rule: **the number of column aliases must match the number of expressions in the query**. A mismatch is a parse error; a count match with reordered SELECT outputs silently produces wrong column-name → value mappings.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nCREATE OR REPLACE VIEW v (a, b, c) AS SELECT x, y, z FROM t;\n\n-- DBSQL — keep the alias list count and intent identical\nCREATE OR REPLACE VIEW my_catalog.my_schema.v (a, b, c) AS\nSELECT x, y, z FROM my_catalog.my_schema.t;\n```\n\nWhen translating, you may need to rewrite the SELECT (UC three-part names, `IFF` → `IF`, `::` casts → `CAST`, etc.). After every such rewrite, re-check:\n\n1. **Same column count** — `len(view_column_list) == len(SELECT_outputs)`.\n2. **Same column order** — the i-th alias in the view header still corresponds to the i-th expression in the SELECT. If you reordered the SELECT (e.g. moved an expression to the front), reorder the alias list to match — never just leave the original alias list in place.\n3. **No silent drops** — if a SELECT expression became unreachable (e.g. `SELECT * EXCEPT(...)` substitution), the alias list must lose the same column.\n\nThree concrete failure modes to scan for after a view rewrite:\n\n- `CREATE VIEW v (a, b, c) AS SELECT col_x, col_y, col_z` where the columns no longer line up with `(a, b, c)` because the SELECT was reshuffled.\n- `CREATE VIEW v (a, b, c) AS SELECT col_x, col_y` (count mismatch — DBSQL rejects).\n- `CREATE VIEW v AS SELECT col_x AS a, col_y AS b, col_z AS c` after dropping the column list — fine, but only if every consumer of the view uses the new aliases.\n\nIf the original Snowflake view had no explicit column list, do not add one in DBSQL.\n\n### Materialized views\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nCREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW sales_by_region AS\nSELECT region, SUM(amount) AS total FROM orders GROUP BY region;\n\n-- DBSQL\nCREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW my_catalog.my_schema.sales_by_region AS\nSELECT region, SUM(amount) AS total FROM my_catalog.my_schema.orders GROUP BY region;\n\n-- Refresh explicitly or on schedule:\nREFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW my_catalog.my_schema.sales_by_region;\n```\n\n**Refresh semantics differ:**\n\n- Snowflake MVs auto-maintain against base-table changes (incremental, background)\n- DBSQL MVs refresh on manual trigger, scheduled trigger, or within a DLT pipeline (incremental inside DLT)\n- DBSQL MVs have restrictions: source tables must be Delta; SQL supported is a subset (no `MATCH_RECOGNIZE`, no user-defined aggregate functions without restrictions, etc.)\n\nIf the Snowflake MV depends on continuous freshness, consider a DLT streaming table / materialized view in a DLT pipeline (see `references/snowflake/07-pipelines.md`).\n\n### Secure views\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nCREATE SECURE VIEW sales_v AS SELECT * FROM sales WHERE region = CURRENT_USER();\n```\n\nDBSQL has no `SECURE VIEW` keyword. Two migration paths:\n\n1. **UC row filters / column masks** on the base table — preferred for data-level security:\n   ```sql\n   CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.region_filter(region STRING)\n   RETURN region = current_user();\n   ALTER TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.sales SET ROW FILTER my_catalog.my_schema.region_filter ON (region);\n   ```\n2. **View + grants** — plain view with `GRANT SELECT ON VIEW` limited to specific principals. Does not prevent definition inspection.\n\n> For UC row filters and column masks, load `skills/writing-sql/row-filters-column-masks.md`.\n\n### Row access policies and masking policies\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nCREATE ROW ACCESS POLICY region_policy AS (region VARCHAR) RETURNS BOOLEAN ->\n  EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM authorized WHERE user_region = region);\nALTER TABLE sales ADD ROW ACCESS POLICY region_policy ON (region);\n\n-- DBSQL: UC row filter\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.region_policy(region STRING)\nRETURN EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM my_catalog.my_schema.authorized WHERE user_region = region);\nALTER TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.sales SET ROW FILTER my_catalog.my_schema.region_policy ON (region);\n\n-- Snowflake masking policy\nCREATE MASKING POLICY email_mask AS (val VARCHAR) RETURNS VARCHAR ->\n  CASE WHEN CURRENT_ROLE() IN ('ANALYST') THEN val ELSE '***' END;\nALTER TABLE users MODIFY COLUMN email SET MASKING POLICY email_mask;\n\n-- DBSQL: UC column mask\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.email_mask(val STRING)\nRETURN CASE WHEN is_member('analyst') THEN val ELSE '***' END;\nALTER TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.users\nALTER COLUMN email SET MASK my_catalog.my_schema.email_mask;\n```\n\nSemantic mapping: Snowflake `CURRENT_ROLE()` ↔ DBSQL `is_member('group_name')` — role is not a 1-to-1 concept.\n\n> Time Travel queries (`AT` / `BEFORE` → `TIMESTAMP AS OF` / `VERSION AS OF`), retention tuning (`DATA_RETENTION_TIME_IN_DAYS` → `delta.*RetentionDuration`), Fail-safe, and zero-copy `CLONE` / `UNDROP` are covered in `references/snowflake/06-time-travel-and-clones.md`.\n"}}]);
